b='content/os/02-threads-scheduling/'
p=b+'sched-linux.md';q=b+'sched-linux.questions.json'
s=['https://docs.kernel.org/scheduler/sched-deadline.html'];m=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/sched.7.html'];f=['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/v6.12/kernel/sched/fair.c'];e=['https://docs.kernel.org/scheduler/sched-ext.html']
edit(p,'A class only gets the CPU if every class above it has nothing runnable.','Ordinary selection considers higher classes first, subject to bandwidth limits. A deadline-class fair server can also dispatch fair work to prevent starvation.', 'Runnable tasks may be throttled and fair-server dispatch is an exception to the simple class diagram.',s)
edit(p,'So a runnable `SCHED_FIFO` thread *always* beats every normal thread, whatever their nice values. Almost everything you run is `SCHED_NORMAL` (called `SCHED_OTHER` in POSIX) in the fair class.','Ignoring throttling and fair-server service, an eligible `SCHED_FIFO` thread outranks normal threads regardless of nice. Ordinary applications usually use `SCHED_NORMAL` (`SCHED_OTHER` in the userspace API). A loaded sched_ext scheduler can take over normal, batch and idle-policy tasks; in partial mode it handles only `SCHED_EXT` tasks.', 'Explain sched_ext full-switch and partial modes.',e)
edit(p,'The ratio was chosen so that one nice step changes a CPU-bound task\'s share by roughly 10%.','For two otherwise equal competitors in one fair group, moving one from nice 0 to nice 1 changes the split from 50/50 to about 55.5/44.5. The share change depends on the competitors.', 'A relative weight ratio is not a universal percentage-point CPU share change.',f)
edit(p,'It is never starved.','That proportional share assumes competition within the same fair group, available CPU bandwidth and no overriding higher-class work.', 'No unconditional starvation guarantee across scheduler classes or quotas.',m)
edit(p,'Three CPU-bound tasks share one CPU:','Three CPU-bound tasks in the same fair group share one CPU:', 'Weight fractions require same scheduling group.',m)
edit(p,'A process with 16 busy threads would get 16 times the CPU of a single-threaded one.','Within one fair group, equal-weight runnable threads compete individually: sixteen such threads collectively receive sixteen shares against one for a single-threaded competitor, when CPU capacity is contested.', 'State assumptions for process aggregate shares.',m)
edit(p,'# Needs root or CAP_SYS_NICE','# Needs CAP_SYS_NICE or sufficient RLIMIT_RTPRIO', 'Resource limits also permit unprivileged realtime scheduling.',m)
edit(p,'Older kernels used RT throttling: by default RT tasks may use 950 ms of every 1 s (`sched_rt_runtime_us` / `sched_rt_period_us`). Newer kernels use a "fair server", a deadline reservation of about 50 ms per second that runs normal tasks if RT work would otherwise starve them.','RT throttling commonly limits RT work to 950 ms per 1 s (`sched_rt_runtime_us` / `sched_rt_period_us`). Linux 6.12 also introduced a deadline-class fair server that can supply CPU to fair tasks. These are distinct mechanisms; behaviour depends on kernel version, configuration and tunables.', 'Fair server does not imply RT bandwidth controls disappeared.',s+f)
edit(p,'You get a guarantee, or you get an error up front.','Admission checks reserve bandwidth; acceptance alone is not a universal proof that application deadlines will be met. Worst-case execution time, blocking, deadline versus period, CPU topology and scheduling assumptions still matter. The kernel documentation distinguishes schedulability tests from the simpler utilization admission test.', 'Kernel docs explicitly show feasible utilization can still miss constrained or multiprocessor deadlines.',s)
edit(p,'- **SCHED_IDLE**: weaker than nice 19, for work that should run only when nothing else wants the CPU.','- **SCHED_IDLE**: extremely low-weight background work in the fair class. It is not the per-CPU idle task and does not promise zero service whenever normal work is runnable.', 'SCHED_IDLE has nonzero fair weight; not strict idle-only policy.',f+m)
edit(p,'Nice 19 still runs; for "only when idle" use `SCHED_IDLE`.','Nice 19 gets a small fair share; `SCHED_IDLE` gives background work an even smaller weight, not a strict idle-only guarantee.', 'Keep pitfall consistent with actual policy.',f)
edit(p,'A higher class always wins.','Bandwidth controls and deadline-class fair-server service qualify this ordering.', 'Avoid contradiction with safety mechanisms.',s)
edit(p,'(about 10% of CPU per step)','(CPU shares depend on competing weights)', 'Weight ratio does not imply fixed CPU percentage change.',f)
edit(p,'SCHED_DEADLINE gives runtime/deadline/period guarantees with admission control.','SCHED_DEADLINE reserves runtime using deadline/period parameters and admission control; application deadline guarantees require additional schedulability assumptions.', 'Admission is not universal hard-deadline proof.',s)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q1','prompt','On one Linux CPU, X is SCHED_FIFO priority 1 and busy-loops. Y is SCHED_NORMAL at nice −20. Ignoring RT throttling, fair-server service and other tasks, which runs?','Both starvation prevention mechanisms must be excluded.',s)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q1','workedExample','X belongs to the real-time class and outranks Y in the fair class. Under the stated exclusions, X remains runnable and Y receives no CPU. On a real system, RT bandwidth limits and fair-server service can change this outcome.','Remove misleading replacement chronology and unconditional class claim.',s)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q3','prompt','Two CPU-bound tasks compete alone in the same fair group on one CPU: a nice 19 backup and a nice 0 service, with no quota or higher-class interference. What share does the backup receive?','State assumptions needed for exact proportions.',m)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q3','text','Roughly 1–2% of the CPU', 'Remove false idle-only recommendation.',f,option=2)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q3','explanation','Correct: 15 / (1024 + 15) is about 1.44%. SCHED_IDLE offers still lower-weight background scheduling, not strict idle-only execution.','Nonzero fair weight.',f,option=2)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q3','workedExample','Nice 0 has weight 1024 and nice 19 has weight 15. The backup share is 15/1039 ≈ 1.44%; the service receives about 98.56%. These are long-run proportions under the stated assumptions. SCHED_IDLE remains in the fair class and does not guarantee execution only when all normal tasks are absent.','Scope proportional result; remove starvation guarantee.',f)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q4','workedExample','Within one fair group, weights 1024 and 820 give shares 1024/1844 ≈ 55.53% and 820/1844 ≈ 44.47%. The gap is about 11.06 percentage points. Each share moved about 5.53 percentage points from 50%; this is not a fixed share change for every possible workload.','Differentiate relative percentage, points and weight.',f)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q7','text','Ignoring throttling and higher scheduling classes, FIFO has no time slice and runs until blocking, yielding or higher RT priority preemption','Qualify listed events.',m,option=1)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q7','workedExample','Options 1, 2 and 3 are true under the stated exclusions. RT priority is 1–99; FIFO has no quantum; RR rotates equals. RR does not demote into fair scheduling. Option 5 is false: setting FIFO requires CAP_SYS_NICE or suitable RLIMIT_RTPRIO permissions, not merely ownership.','Original worked step labelled different proposition false.',m)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q8','explanation','Without sufficient RLIMIT_NICE or privilege, lowering the numeric nice value fails rather than silently succeeding. Assume the requested change succeeds here.','Negative nice not always privileged.',m,option=1)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q8','explanation','The question assumes CPU-bound encoding and build work. Group scheduling explains why changing an individual thread weight may not change the group share.','Encoding is not inherently always CPU-bound.',m,option=2)
qe(q,'sched-linux-q10','workedExample','The requested total utilization is 30/100 + 40/100 + 12/40 = 1.00, above the configured 0.95 admission limit, so the request is rejected with EBUSY, assuming otherwise valid parameters and permissions. A request of 10/40 makes utilization 0.95 and passes this bandwidth check. Passing admission does not alone prove all application deadlines will be met.','Remove unconditional guarantee and distinguish check from complete syscall validation.',s)
